WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.524
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.824 --> 00:00:08.909
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:19.175
Unit forty-five: Law, Morality and Conscience. In this unit, you will debate the relationship between law and morality.

4
00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:21.393
natural law.

5
00:00:22.093 --> 00:00:28.296
natural law means the theory that some moral rules are universal and above human-made law.

6
00:00:28.596 --> 00:00:32.836
Natural law says some acts are wrong whatever the statute book says.

7
00:00:33.536 --> 00:00:36.978
Your turn. Say: natural law.

8
00:00:39.678 --> 00:00:40.865
natural law.

9
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.362
legal positivism.

10
00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:51.541
legal positivism means the theory that law is simply the rules made by legitimate authorities, whether moral or not.

11
00:00:51.841 --> 00:00:56.918
Legal positivism separates what the law is from what it ought to be.

12
00:00:57.618 --> 00:01:01.361
Your turn. Say: legal positivism.

13
00:01:04.061 --> 00:01:05.805
legal positivism.

14
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:08.259
moral conscience.

15
00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:13.489
moral conscience means a person's inner sense of right and wrong.

16
00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:17.136
Her moral conscience would not let her sign the false report.

17
00:01:17.836 --> 00:01:21.278
Your turn. Say: moral conscience.

18
00:01:23.978 --> 00:01:25.327
moral conscience.

19
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:27.457
unjust law.

20
00:01:28.157 --> 00:01:32.966
unjust law means a law that violates basic fairness or human rights.

21
00:01:33.266 --> 00:01:38.075
Martin Luther King wrote that an unjust law is no law at all.

22
00:01:38.775 --> 00:01:42.055
Your turn. Say: unjust law.

23
00:01:44.755 --> 00:01:46.069
unjust law.

24
00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:49.269
rule of law ideal.

25
00:01:49.969 --> 00:01:57.239
rule of law ideal means the principle that everyone, including rulers, is equally subject to the law.

26
00:01:57.539 --> 00:02:01.466
The rule of law ideal means no one is above the law.

27
00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:05.190
Your turn. Say: rule of law ideal.

28
00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.307
rule of law ideal.

29
00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:12.562
legal ethics.

30
00:02:13.262 --> 00:02:17.188
legal ethics means the moral duties of lawyers and judges.

31
00:02:17.488 --> 00:02:20.788
Legal ethics forbid a lawyer from lying to the court.

32
00:02:21.488 --> 00:02:24.686
Your turn. Say: legal ethics.

33
00:02:27.386 --> 00:02:28.666
legal ethics.

34
00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.243
conscientious judgement.

35
00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:36.404
conscientious judgement means a careful decision guided by moral principle.

36
00:02:36.704 --> 00:02:41.188
Judges must apply conscientious judgement, not personal preference.

37
00:02:41.888 --> 00:02:45.899
Your turn. Say: conscientious judgement.

38
00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:50.111
conscientious judgement.

39
00:02:51.117 --> 00:02:52.547
jurisprudence.

40
00:02:53.247 --> 00:02:56.559
jurisprudence means the theory and philosophy of law.

41
00:02:56.859 --> 00:03:02.074
In jurisprudence, the debate between natural law and positivism continues.

42
00:03:02.774 --> 00:03:05.937
Your turn. Say: jurisprudence.

43
00:03:08.637 --> 00:03:09.894
jurisprudence.

44
00:03:10.883 --> 00:03:13.150
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:16.750
Ms. Sarah, must we obey every law?

46
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:22.720
Well, the law and morality are not always on the best of terms.

47
00:03:24.017 --> 00:03:26.028
That sounds like an understatement.

48
00:03:27.317 --> 00:03:32.314
It is, slightly. History offers a few unjust laws, shall we say.

49
00:03:33.617 --> 00:03:36.754
Like apartheid or the segregation laws in America?

50
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:43.610
Exactly. Legal, yes. Moral? Not entirely, one might gently suggest.

51
00:03:44.917 --> 00:03:47.520
So natural law beats legal positivism?

52
00:03:48.817 --> 00:03:55.242
A positivist would raise an eyebrow at that. They'd say: identify the law first, judge it second.

53
00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:01.754
Grammar focus: Irony, understatement and tone (extended).

54
00:04:02.054 --> 00:04:04.715
Understatement can carry strong criticism.

55
00:04:05.015 --> 00:04:12.973
The law and morality are not always on the best of terms. History offers a few unjust laws, shall we say.

56
00:04:13.273 --> 00:04:16.680
Moral? Not entirely, one might gently suggest.

57
00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.682
Ironic understatement lets you make a strong criticism sound calm; your tone of voice must signal the irony.

58
00:04:24.683 --> 00:04:28.323
Now read and listen. When Law and Conscience Collide.

59
00:04:28.623 --> 00:04:32.271
What is the relationship between law and morality?

60
00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:36.500
This question lies at the heart of jurisprudence.

61
00:04:37.817 --> 00:04:47.409
Supporters of natural law argue that genuine law must respect universal moral principles; an unjust law therefore lacks true authority.

62
00:04:48.717 --> 00:04:59.675
Legal positivism, associated with thinkers such as H. L. A. Hart, replies that law and morality are conceptually separate.

63
00:05:00.983 --> 00:05:07.906
Both camps, however, accept the rule of law ideal: rulers, too, must obey the law.

64
00:05:09.217 --> 00:05:15.315
History records, to put it mildly, several occasions when following the law meant committing injustice.

65
00:05:16.617 --> 00:05:22.134
In such moments, individuals must rely on moral conscience and conscientious judgement.

66
00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:30.697
Legal ethics, meanwhile, remind lawyers and judges that their duty is to justice, not merely to procedure.

67
00:05:31.983 --> 00:05:34.624
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:34.924 --> 00:05:39.733
The theory that some moral rules are above human-made law is blank.

69
00:05:40.033 --> 00:05:46.102
A, natural law. B, legal positivism. C, jurisprudence.

70
00:05:50.117 --> 00:05:52.267
The answer is A: natural law.

71
00:05:53.783 --> 00:05:58.314
'The meeting was not exactly a success' most likely means it was blank.

72
00:05:58.614 --> 00:06:03.637
A, a failure. B, a great success. C, on time.

73
00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.044
The answer is A: a failure.

74
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.639
'History offers a few unjust laws, shall we say' uses blank.

75
00:06:15.939 --> 00:06:21.636
A, ironic understatement. B, exaggeration. C, a question.

76
00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:29.031
The answer is A: ironic understatement.

77
00:06:30.517 --> 00:06:32.969
The philosophy of law is blank.

78
00:06:33.269 --> 00:06:39.140
A, jurisprudence. B, legal ethics. C, moral conscience.

79
00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:45.939
The answer is A: jurisprudence.

80
00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:52.410
According to the text, which thinker is associated with legal positivism?

81
00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:59.587
A, H. L. A. Hart. B, Martin Luther King. C, Socrates.

82
00:07:03.583 --> 00:07:07.379
The answer is A: H. L. A. Hart.

83
00:07:08.883 --> 00:07:13.335
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is it ever right to break the law?

84
00:07:18.635 --> 00:07:25.012
Part two: Use one ironic understatement and refer to natural law or legal positivism.

85
00:07:31.312 --> 00:07:46.029
Segregation laws were, shall we say, not entirely fair. Natural law theorists would say an unjust law is no law at all. So yes, peaceful civil disobedience can be justified, but only as a last resort.

86
00:07:46.329 --> 00:07:48.155
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:55.297
Great work today! You learned eight new words and irony, understatement and tone (extended).

88
00:07:55.597 --> 00:07:59.642
Today's value is fair play. Play fairly and follow the rules.

89
00:07:59.942 --> 00:08:03.558
See you in Unit forty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:08:03.858 --> 00:08:06.847
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

